    {
      "topic_id": "bearings_cosine_rule_H",
      "prompt": "A ship sails from port A on a bearing of 031° for 12 km to point B. It then changes course and sails on a bearing of 354° for 27 km to point C. Find the direct distance from A to C, correct to 2 decimal places.",
      "solution_steps": [
        "The back bearing of A from B is 031° + 180° = 211°.",
        "Angle ABC is the difference between this back bearing and the bearing of C from B: angle ABC = 143°.",
        "Use the cosine rule: AC² = AB² + BC² - 2×AB×BC×cos(ABC)",
        "AC² = 12² + 27² - 2×12×27×cos(143°) = 1391",
        "AC = √1391 = 37.29 km (2 d.p.)"
      ],
      "final_answer": "37.29 km",
      "marks": 5,
      "mark_scheme": [
        {
          "code": "M1",
          "description": "The back bearing of A from B is 031° + 180° = 211°.",
          "marks": 1
        },
